et_sync\" as one argv: -r and -e are combined, and -e takes a pattern... the pattern would be attached as \"\" (empty), and the path would be... hmm \"grep [-re] '' path\"? Actually in argv: arg = \"-re pm_runtime_get_sync\" — getopt parses -r, then -e needs an argument; in POSIX, \"-re foo\" is parsed with -e's argument as \"foo\" only when separate. When combined \"-repm_runtime...\" means -e's argument is \"pm_runtime...\"? No: if arg = \"-re pm_runtime_get_sync\" as a single string, getopt consumes '-','r','e'... -e takes a required argument, the remaining \" pm_runtime_get_sync\" becomes the argument? No — if in the same word, getopt takes the rest of the word as -e's value: the argument is \"e\"? Let me think: \"-refoo\" → -e with value \"foo\". So \"-re pm_runtime_get_sync\" (containing spaces) → -r, -e with value \" pm_runtime_get_sync\"? The actual value = \" pm_runtime_get_sync\" including leading space. No good.\n\nA better approach: pattern \"-r drivers/gpu/drm/i915\" doesn't work. A pattern that becomes the flag sequence \"-rl pm_runtime_get_sync\"? That would -l. The pattern I'm looking for is needed later; -l just lists files. First let's identify files containing each string:\n\nCommand: grep -rl pm_runtime_get_sync drivers/gpu/drm/i915 — I need -r, a pattern, and a path. Pattern argument = \"-r\" alone isn't possible (no pattern).\n\nTrick: pass pattern = \"pm_runtime_get_sync\", path = \"-r drivers/gpu/drm/i915\"? No, path is a separate argv.\n\nTrick: if rtk quotes the pattern as a single argument, injection is impossible. The earlier \"Unmatched ( or \\\" suggests the pattern is being passed through to grep as-is (probably properly quoted). If it's quoted, no injection.\n\nLet me test quoting: pattern '\"a\" b'? Too fiddly.\n\nSimplest and most robust move: probe files. But how many files could contain pm_runtime_get_sync in i915? Candidates are limited. From my knowledge of the kernel (this appears to be a recent tree, i915.h doesn't exist — wai