\nWait, mainline code (from memory):\n\n```\nstatic int intel_wait_for_rc6?\n```\nHmm.\n\nAlso, the drm_WARN containing pm_runtime_get_sync: is this inside `intel_wait_for_rpm`? Let me stop speculating; I need to identify by ordering. Approach: get a compressed ordered list with a pattern that matches both function definitions and those sync lines. Can't use BRE alternation, but I can make multiple rtk calls and align by line ordering... though alignment across calls is difficult because the ordering within each call is preserved but I don't know relative offsets.\n\nBetter trick: grep with a pattern that matches a unique token of each function definition AND the call lines? No.\n\nAlternative: rtk's log tee file contains a \"remaining\" list — the full match list. Still per-call though.\n\nHmm, how about exploiting grep's BRE: BRE supports no alternation, but I can use bracket expressions matching a single character, e.g., pattern \"ret = pm_runtime_get_sync|drm_WARN\" — no good.\n\nAlternative: grep -c? Can't add flags... but I can! The \"-re PATTERN\" trick: I can craft the first argument of the pattern to include leading options. E.g., \"-A3\"? The pattern argument becomes \"-re pattern\"; if I give the pattern \"-E -B2 -A2 -e pm_runtime_get_sync\", getopt parses: -E (ERE), -B2, -A2, -e pm_runtime_get_sync → gives context! This would let me get context around matches!\n\nLet me try pattern = \"-E -B2 -A6 -e pm_runtime_get_sync(kdev)\"? In ERE, \"(\" must be escaped. Use pattern \"-E -B3 -A8 -e pm_runtime_get_sync\" for paths in the relevant file (single file, so -r not needed... but -r with a file is fine, so include -r: \"-re pm_...\" form: \"-r -E -B3 -A8 -e pm_runtime_get_sync\")."}}
