r -r, the next character becomes another option and is a space. And the reason the earlier \"-re pm_runtime_get_sync\" worked is because '-re pm_...' is parsed as -r, -e + rest of the word = \" pm_...\" — no invalid character until the option characters are consumed: after -e consumes the rest of the word, there are no more option characters.\n\nSo: options must be packed together without spaces, and the pattern for -e must be inside the same word. Format: \"-rBA3ePATTERN\"? -B requires a value: -BA3 — is a valid combined form \"-BA3\" valid? POSIX combined options: -B requires a required argument; combining \"-BA3\", B's arg becomes \"A3\"?? No — the value ends up as \"A3\", which is invalid. Hmm: actually does getopt support -B=A? As a combined form \"-B=A3\"? Nonstandard, but GNU getopt handles \"-B=\" only if long... no, it's short.\n\nAlternative: use -e only (works: rest of word becomes the pattern) + -r. \"-rePATTERN\" is already in use. What about context? GNU short options: -B, -A require separate arguments. Can they not be combined?\n\nOptions that don't take a value and can be combined: -r, -E, -l, -c, -n, -o, -w, -q, -h... -n is useful! \"-rne PATTERN\" → -r -n -e PATTERN? Wait: \"-rne pm_runtime_get_sync\": characters after '-': r(none), n(none), e(takes rest = \" pm_runtime_get_sync\"). -n = line number! With line numbers I can identify function positions!\n\nLet me redo the main grep with line numbers using -rne.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"The argv is passed whole, so options must be concatenated: `-rne` = `-r -n -e`. Let me get line numbers:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_nx932kfj","name":"rtk","arguments":{"pattern":"-rne pm_runtime_get_sync","path":"drivers/gpu/drm/i915/intel_runtime_pm.c"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_7rb5sxsm","name":"rtk","arguments":{"pattern":"-rne static intel_wakeref_t|^int","path":"drivers/gpu/drm/i915/intel_runtime_pm.c"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"ollama","model":"qwen3.8:27b