{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"No \"static int \" or \"static void \" exist in the file?! Yet just now I saw lines like \"static void init_intel_runtime_pm_wakeref(struct intel_runtime_pm *rpm)\". So \"static void \" should match those lines... but wait, the pattern I searched for was \"-rne static int \" — getopt should parse -r -n -e \"static int \" (the -e value includes a trailing space; a pattern containing a trailing space should still match without issue). Hmm, that \"static void init_...\" line from earlier came from a grep of the \"intel_runtime_pm\" pattern, and it displayed \"static void init_intel_runtime_pm_wakeref(struct intel_runtime_pm *rpm)\" — so \"static void \" definitely exists! Yet grep \"static void \" fails??\n\nMaybe... is rtk trimming trailing spaces off the pattern? \"-rne static int \" → value \"static int \" gets trimmed → \"static int\" (no space) — even then it should match! \"static int\" is a substring...\n\nHmm wait, there's no \"static void init_intel_runtime_pm_wakeref\"? The grep output from earlier did show that line. So the \"static void\" substring exists. Why no match?!\n\nCould it be that rtk is interpreting the pattern as an ERE? If \"-rne\" is passing -E... no, -E isn't being passed.\n\nOr maybe rtk is caching/deduplicating?? The tool output \"Command failed\" = grep exit code 1 or 2. Exit code 1 means no match. Strange.\n\nMaybe the issue is this: rtk runs \"grep '-rne static int ' file\"? That is, it's wrapping the entire pattern in quotes → single-argument pattern \"-rne static int \" is the PATTERN (no options) → as a BRE it matches the literal \"-rne static int \" → no match. But the earlier \"-rne pm_runtime_get_sync\" did work with line numbers (line numbers were produced!). So options do pass through... the difference: the working pattern had exactly one space: \"-rne pm_runtime_get_sync\". The failing ones have two spaces (\"-rne static int \" has \"-rne\" + \"static int \" → a total of two spaces: one after -rne, another as a trailing space). Maybe rtk splits the pattern on the first space: first token = flags, rest = pattern. \"-rne\" and \"static int \" (trailing space) — a pattern with trailing space should still match... hmm.\n\nUnless rtk trims whitespace on both ends of the pattern! \"static int \" → \"static int\" → no, that should still match.\n\nWait, maybe rtk also splits the pattern on the second space?? So pattern = \"static\", and the rest \"int\" gets passed as an additional argument? grep would then treat \"int\" as a second file → \"grep: int: No such file\"? But then the error message should show that. The output was just \"Command failed\".\n\nLet me test: pattern \"-rne static\" — single space. If it matches, that would confirm trailing-space/splitting weirdness."}}
